  "worked_solution": "The center of the expansion is $x=-3,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-1,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $2,$ i.e., $R\\ge 2.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-5,-1].$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-5,-1].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/assets/7d68a4bd81717c3ad663fc85c45dfce62217a046f683cc92a6869f87ff3b166a.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $-5\\notin (-5,-1],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $-2\\in (-5,-1],$ the series is convergent at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $1\\notin (-5,-1],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
